.TH voltkit-expander 1  "Voltkit 0.7.0" "Voltkit Manual"
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH NAME
voltkit\-expander \- Set up and toggle typed triggers (type ;date anywhere to expand it)
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBvoltkit expander\fR [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fIsubcommands\fR>
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH DESCRIPTION
Set up and toggle typed triggers (type ;date anywhere to expand it)
.PP
Typed triggers read keyboard input through evdev and paste through a uinput virtual keyboard, which needs a one\-time setup with root rights. Only the last few typed characters are kept in memory; nothing is logged or stored.
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
\fBvoltkit expander status\fR
Show whether typed triggers are enabled and can run
.TP
\fBvoltkit expander setup [\-\-apply]\fR
Grant keyboard access and turn typed triggers on, right away (no logout needed)
.RS
\fB\-\-apply\fR: Run the commands (asks for the administrator password) instead of only printing them
.RE
.TP
\fBvoltkit expander enable\fR
Turn typed triggers on
.TP
\fBvoltkit expander disable\fR
Turn typed triggers off
.TP
\fBvoltkit expander remove\-access\fR
Turn typed triggers off and remove Voltkit\*(Aqs keyboard access
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH "HOW IT WORKS"
.PP
The daemon reads key presses from /dev/input and keeps the last 64 typed characters in memory. When they end with a snippet\*(Aqs trigger, it erases the trigger with Backspace through a uinput virtual keyboard and pastes the expansion, restoring the clipboard afterwards. Snippets with {arg:NAME} parameters open the picker\*(Aqs form instead.
.PP
Setup installs a udev rule that tags keyboards and /dev/uinput with uaccess, the way desktops grant devices to the person at the computer, and applies it at once: no group to join and no need to log out. The daemon starts and stops typed triggers as they are turned on and off, and picks up keyboards plugged in later.
.PP
Keys are decoded with a US layout, so triggers made of letters, digits and ; work on most layouts while punctuation may differ. A trigger that is a prefix of another (;d and ;date) fires first, so the longer one is unreachable.
.SH SECURITY
.PP
Keyboard access applies to every program the person at the computer runs, not only Voltkit. Typed text is never logged, written to disk or sent anywhere. `voltkit expander remove\-access` (or turning typed triggers off in the app and choosing to remove access) deletes /etc/udev/rules.d/70\-voltkit\-typed\-triggers.rules and /etc/modules\-load.d/voltkit\-uinput.conf; the access already granted ends at the next restart. Setups from before version 0.6 also added you to the input group, which `gpasswd \-d $USER input` undoes.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Review the setup commands, then run them:
.RS 4
.nf
\fBvoltkit expander setup
voltkit expander setup \-\-apply\fR
.fi
.RE
.PP
Turn typed triggers off, and remove the keyboard access:
.RS 4
.nf
\fBvoltkit expander remove\-access\fR
.fi
.RE
